      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "My dual-core keeps getting more and more mature. I’m already getting over 2 CoreMark/MHz out of it, and I’m going to turn it into a quad-core next. Then I think the project is basically done.\n\nThe only thing that sucks is that I can’t turn it into an ASIC. I need a smaller process node.\n\nI really wanted to build an SMP Linux chip. <- @Tim 'mithro' Ansell   \uD83D\uDE42\n\nhttps://x.com/splinedrive/status/2095241420315795911",
      "author": {
        "id": "840182560252624916",
        "name": "logic_destroyer",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "asic destroyer",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-09_media/6f21ec45bc9221220509b769f634344b-E96BD.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [
        {
          "title": "",
          "url": "https://twitter.com/splinedrive/status/2095241420315795911",
          "timestamp": "2026-09-02T20:04:16.963+00:00",
          "description": "Bro, what the hell did I build?\n\nMy KianV RISC\\-V Linux/NetBSD SoC is running Linux 7\\.2\\.2 now\\.\n\nI can run desktop apps with an analog clock whose second hand actually keeps perfect time, listen to 256 kbps MP3s, write and compile C/C\\+\\+ natively with GCC/G\\+\\+, and even play Doom while listening to MP3…",
          "color": "#1DA1F2",
          "author": {
            "name": "Logic Destroyer (@splinedrive)",
            "url": "https://x.com/splinedrive"
          },
